From da74e3570a9d0ce70d4cf63038e9700d8a5112ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheCryptoDonkey <TheCryptoDonkey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 09:25:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] feat: log in to the dashboard with a linked signer

A linked identity can now be enrolled to log in to the dashboard through
its NIP-46 signer. The password stays and always works; enrolment never
replaces it, and removing an enrolment never locks anyone out.

auth.signer.enrol.start takes { identity_id, password }. It re-checks
the password, accepts only an identity linked to a signer, and asks the
signer to sign a login challenge, returning { attempt_id, code } so the
dashboard can show the code to check against the device.
auth.signer.enrol.finish { attempt_id } waits at most 10 s and returns
{ status: "pending" }, or saves the enrolment once the signer approves
(re-checking that the identity is still linked to the same key) and
returns it with approval_ms and quick_approval, set when the signer
answered in under a second, which suggests one that signs without
asking. Both are bound to the owner's session. auth.signer.list lists
enrolments; auth.signer.remove takes { identity_id, password }. One
signer can be enrolled for now: a second is refused until the first is
removed. Enrolments are kept in user.json beside the TOTP data, written
through a synced temporary file that keeps the file's permissions.
Deleting an identity removes its enrolment first. These methods need a
session and follow the dashboard origin rule.

A login sends the enrolled signer a kind 22242 event to sign: a 32-byte
random challenge tag, a relay tag with the dashboard origin the browser
used and a code tag with four random digits, content "Log in to <node
name>". The page shows the code so the owner can check their signer
shows the same before pressing.

Without 2FA, auth.login.signer.available is true once a signer is
enrolled, and auth.login.signer.start {} returns { attempt_id, code }
with no password. auth.login.signer.finish { attempt_id } waits at most
10 s and returns { status: "pending" }, or mints the session, CSRF and
remember cookies exactly as a password login does.

With 2FA, the signer stands in for the TOTP code, not the password.
available is false; auth.login with the right password returns
requires_totp as before, now with signer: true when one is enrolled,
and leaves its pending session. start then needs that pending session
and is bound to it, and an approved finish upgrades it as
auth.login.totp does. The signer login must be started from the browser
that gave the password. Checking the pending session does not count as a
TOTP attempt. Without it, start gives the same answer as a node with no
signer, so nothing before the password says whether 2FA is on beyond
whether a password-less signer login is offered.

The signed event is checked when it arrives: kind, enrolled key,
signature, challenge, relay and code (each exactly once), and
created_at within 60 s of the node's clock. It goes through the linked
signer session, so an offline, refusing or slow signer gives a clear
error without hanging. An unauthenticated caller sees only fixed texts
(offline, denied, no answer in time, refused, failed); the detail,
which can include the signer's relays or its own words, is logged.

An attempt is single use and lasts two minutes in all; the signer has
about a minute of that to answer. It belongs to whoever started it:
without 2FA, start sets an HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict cookie on /rpc/,
named for the attempt, that finish must present, cleared once the
attempt is used up or gone; otherwise the session or pending session.
A finish without it is refused and leaves the attempt alone. While a
login or enrolment waits on a signer, or is approved but not yet
finished, another start for that signer is refused rather than
replacing it, so nobody can keep cancelling the owner's request and the
device never shows both kinds at once. The refusal tells the owner that
a prompt on their signer did not come from their page and to deny it.
A failed attempt frees the slot at once.

Login starts and finishes, and enrolment starts and finishes, are
refused from any browser origin but the dashboard: the same host as the
request's Host, on port 80 or 443. So an app page on the node cannot
start a login, read its code and show it to the owner, and other sites
cannot start one at all. Requests without Origin (non-browser clients)
are allowed. A dashboard reached on another port, such as through a
port-forward to 8443, cannot use signer login or enrolment and falls
back to the password.

Every login start counts against the password login rate limit (five a
minute per address), as does every failed finish. On top of that,
logins have a node-wide budget of three starts in ten minutes, and each
failed, denied or expired login pauses the next start, for longer each
time (30 seconds, two minutes, ten minutes). This caps how often anyone
can make the signer ask. On a node without 2FA, someone who can reach
the login page can keep signer login paused for as long as they keep
trying; password login always works, and a successful password or
signer login clears the pause. auth.signer.status (for the logged-in
owner, from the dashboard) returns when signer login is paused until
and the last 20 login starts with their address and outcome (pending, approved,
denied, expired, offline, refused, failed, refused-busy,
refused-budget), so the dashboard can say who tripped a pause. The
record is kept in memory only. A password-less login is also refused at
finish if 2FA was switched on while the signer was asking. The new
methods have endpoint limits too.

NIP-46 connect now carries the client metadata { "name": "Archipelago" }
as its fourth param, with empty strings for an absent secret and for
requested permissions, as nostr-tools sends them. Before, a bunker://
link sent only the signer key and secret, so a signer had no name to
show for the node and showed a bare client key instead.
